<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shang Yang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 390–<a href="/years/338-bce">338 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Lord Shang, Wei Yang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shang Yang was a Chinese statesman and Legalist thinker, a nobleman of the State of Wey, who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-xiao-of-qin">Duke Xiao of Qin</a> from 361 BCE. His reforms abolished hereditary privilege, rewarded farming and military merit, imposed harsh laws with collective responsibility and organised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> into counties. They turned Qin into a centralised war state that later unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. After Duke Xiao's death he was executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shang-yang.jpg" alt="Shang Yang" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Taken by Fanghong · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Shang_Yang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
